WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.800 --> 00:00:04.400
Christopher Mintz-Plasse first&nbsp;
gained fame by cracking audiences&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:04.400 --> 00:00:09.440
up as McLovin in 2007's Superbad. He's&nbsp;
continued working steadily since then, but a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:09.440 --> 00:00:14.560
lot of fans haven't seen him on screen very much&nbsp;
lately. Keep watching to discover why that is.

00:00:15.440 --> 00:00:19.920
Ever since he obtained the world's fakest&nbsp;
fake ID and charmed a couple of bored police&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:19.920 --> 00:00:24.560
officers in Superbad, Mintz-Plasse&nbsp;
has forever been known as McLovin.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:24.560 --> 00:00:30.000
Even though he's gone on to star in other notable&nbsp;
movies like Role Models, Kick-Ass, and Neighbors,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:30.000 --> 00:00:35.040
his first role is still undoubtedly his most&nbsp;
well known. That's a fine state of affairs&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:35.040 --> 00:00:39.680
for actors who are satisfied with being cult&nbsp;
figures forever, but it's not so great if you&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:39.680 --> 00:00:45.600
want to have a robust and steady mainstream&nbsp;
career. Unfortunately for Mintz-Plasse then,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:45.600 --> 00:00:50.800
it's hard for audiences and studio executives&nbsp;
to see him as anything other than McLovin.

00:00:50.800 --> 00:00:56.000
It's no exaggeration to say that Superbad has&nbsp;
overshadowed every single thing Mintz-Plasse has&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:56.000 --> 00:01:01.680
done since . It's a fair bet that at least half&nbsp;
his fans don't even recognize him by his real&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:01.680 --> 00:01:07.840
name. And while the cachet of that performance&nbsp;
continues to draw crowds for his other projects,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:07.840 --> 00:01:11.840
it isn't helping him branch out or&nbsp;
move forward into more serious roles.

00:01:13.200 --> 00:01:14.240
"I am McLovin!"

00:01:15.280 --> 00:01:20.800
Between maturing into adulthood and losing his&nbsp;
baby chipmunk cheeks, Mintz-Plasse has followed&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:20.800 --> 00:01:26.640
in the footsteps of so many teen movie nerd actors&nbsp;
before him by becoming a conventionally attractive&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:26.640 --> 00:01:32.400
man. And yet nobody seems to realize it, including&nbsp;
Hollywood, and perhaps even Mintz-Plasse himself.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:32.400 --> 00:01:37.760
So for whoever needs to hear it, here's the&nbsp;
truth: Christopher Mintz-Plasse is a handsome man!

00:01:37.760 --> 00:01:43.280
With his comedy skills and very pleasant face,&nbsp;
he could easily hold his own as an everyman&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:43.280 --> 00:01:48.880
romantic lead, or he could just as easily play&nbsp;
the part of the likable best friend. But instead,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:48.880 --> 00:01:54.160
his potential as a perfectly respectable leading&nbsp;
man is hidden under a steady stream of typecasting&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:54.160 --> 00:01:59.040
and voice-over roles. All he needs is a big,&nbsp;
juicy part that declares his newfound image&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:59.040 --> 00:02:04.720
to the world, but it just hasn't arrived yet. To&nbsp;
sum it up, Mintz-Plasse is probably dealing with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:04.720 --> 00:02:09.760
the double-edged sword that belongs to former&nbsp;
child stars who played one major role early in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:09.760 --> 00:02:15.120
their careers. It's as if he needs to look like&nbsp;
his 17-year-old self in order to be recognizable&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:15.120 --> 00:02:20.400
and land roles, but he's aged out of those roles&nbsp;
that his original reputation would help him land.

00:02:21.200 --> 00:02:28.000
In May 2012, Mintz-Plasse scored potentially his&nbsp;
biggest post-Superbad role yet when CBS announced&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:28.000 --> 00:02:34.000
that it had ordered a sitcom called Friend Me. He&nbsp;
was set to star in it alongside Nicholas Braun as&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:34.000 --> 00:02:39.120
two guys who leave their Indiana hometown&nbsp;
in favor of Los Angeles and jobs at Groupon.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:39.120 --> 00:02:43.200
Mintz-Plasse played the nerdy one who's&nbsp;
content to hang out at home talking to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:43.200 --> 00:02:47.520
his old friends online. The show seemed poised&nbsp;
to be the perfect transitional vehicle for him,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:47.520 --> 00:02:52.480
as a major network series could've been a&nbsp;
springboard to all kinds of big-deal roles.

00:02:52.480 --> 00:02:56.800
But tragically, Friend Me creator Alan&nbsp;
Kirschenbaum killed himself before the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:56.800 --> 00:03:02.320
show premiered. CBS decided to leave the show out&nbsp;
of its lineup and never aired a single one of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:02.320 --> 00:03:07.200
eight episodes that had already been produced.&nbsp;
Obviously, a blow to one's to career plans&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:07.200 --> 00:03:13.245
pales in comparison to death, but this was&nbsp;
nevertheless a tough break for Mintz-Plasse.

00:03:13.280 --> 00:03:17.280
Mintz-Plasse's Superbad castmates have&nbsp;
largely gone on to have high-profile careers.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:17.280 --> 00:03:22.720
Michael Cera has starred in cult hits like Scott&nbsp;
Pilgrim vs. the World and Juno, while Jonah Hill&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:22.720 --> 00:03:28.640
has transitioned between traditional comedies and&nbsp;
awards-baiting films like The Wolf of Wall Street.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:28.640 --> 00:03:32.960
Some of them have even won Oscars, as Emma&nbsp;
Stone took home the gold for La La Land.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:32.960 --> 00:03:37.200
In comparison, Mintz-Plasse's public&nbsp;
profile is nowhere near as high.

00:03:37.200 --> 00:03:42.640
Being in big movies means playing the Hollywood&nbsp;
glad-handing game, but Mintz-Plasse just doesn't&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:42.640 --> 00:03:47.840
seem to be too interested in all that. Sure, he'll&nbsp;
occasionally pose with friends and co-stars on the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:47.840 --> 00:03:53.200
red carpet before a movie premiere, but other than&nbsp;
that, his media presence is notably low-key. His&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:53.200 --> 00:03:58.160
Instagram followers are far more likely to see him&nbsp;
cuddling with dogs or goofing off in his kitchen&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:58.160 --> 00:04:03.600
than hanging out at an exclusive nightclub. His&nbsp;
personal life is just that: personal. He also&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:03.600 --> 00:04:08.960
doesn't get much attention for his love life, thus&nbsp;
avoiding any tumultuous, tabloid-ready affairs.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:08.960 --> 00:04:13.840
In short, he's just not chasing the kind of fame&nbsp;
that might make him more of a household name.

00:04:14.160 --> 00:04:20.240
After the cancellation of Friend Me, Mintz-Plasse&nbsp;
got another chance to star on another CBS sitcom&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:20.240 --> 00:04:25.280
about people in their twenties working for an&nbsp;
internet company. Entitled The Great Indoors,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:25.280 --> 00:04:30.800
it also starred Joel McHale and Stephen Fry as&nbsp;
the editor and publisher of an outdoors magazine,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:30.800 --> 00:04:36.960
while Mintz-Plasse played a nerdy computer&nbsp;
whiz. The show finished the 2016-to-2017 season&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:36.960 --> 00:04:42.720
as the 38th-most-watched show on network TV. That&nbsp;
may not sound too shabby, but it was apparently&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:42.720 --> 00:04:47.680
disappointing to CBS, as it was cancelled&nbsp;
after just one season. It certainly didn't&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:47.680 --> 00:04:52.880
help that critics also hated the series, with&nbsp;
The Hollywood Reporter for one calling it, quote,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:52.880 --> 00:04:57.200
"a malaise of millennial minstrelsy."&nbsp;
Being associated with a failed sitcom&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:57.200 --> 00:05:01.120
given every chance to succeed can't&nbsp;
be a good look for anyone's career.

00:05:01.840 --> 00:05:04.760
"Oh, let me help you with this guy here. Oh!"

00:05:05.680 --> 00:05:09.200
Around the same time that The Great&nbsp;
Indoors was underwhelming viewers,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:09.200 --> 00:05:14.320
Mintz-Plasse appeared in another quickly&nbsp;
forgotten TV series. Flaked was a Netflix&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:14.320 --> 00:05:19.600
dramedy that premiered in 2017 about addiction&nbsp;
recovery that starred Will Arnett. Once again,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:19.600 --> 00:05:24.080
Mintz-Plasse played a socially awkward&nbsp;
techie. The show lasted a mere two seasons,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:24.080 --> 00:05:27.440
with Mintz-Plasse showing up in&nbsp;
three episodes of the first season.

00:05:28.800 --> 00:05:33.520
Mintz-Plasse isn't just an actor. He's also a&nbsp;
dedicated musician, and his projects on that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:33.520 --> 00:05:38.320
front are clearly just as important to him as&nbsp;
being on screen. He's spending a lot of his&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:38.320 --> 00:05:43.120
time these days practicing, performing, and&nbsp;
recording with his indie rock band, MainMan.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:43.120 --> 00:05:46.400
While he's definitely the most famous&nbsp;
member of that group outside of music,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:46.400 --> 00:05:51.440
he doesn't have the showiest role in the&nbsp;
band, as he strums away on the bass guitar.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:51.440 --> 00:05:55.680
Still, he knows where to find his audience,&nbsp;
as the band debuted their very 70s-style&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:55.680 --> 00:06:02.560
video for its Steely Dan-esque single "WWH"&nbsp;
on Funny or Die's YouTube channel in 2017.

00:06:02.560 --> 00:06:07.520
And MainMan isn't even Mintz-Plasse's only&nbsp;
musical project, as he drummed for a group&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:07.520 --> 00:06:13.680
called The Young Rapscallions and played bass&nbsp;
for another band called Bear on Fire. Splitting&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:13.680 --> 00:06:18.880
his time this way is exactly what he wants&nbsp;
to keep doing. In 2015, he told Stereogum,

00:06:18.880 --> 00:06:23.440
"I would love to keep balancing the two. I think&nbsp;
I'm very lucky and grateful for where I am right&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:23.440 --> 00:06:28.720
now, that I can do both. Acting, you can work&nbsp;
for three months and not work for six months.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:28.720 --> 00:06:32.640
So in that time, I love the momentum of&nbsp;
music and everything that's going on.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:32.640 --> 00:06:36.560
The momentum right now is very musical.&nbsp;
I kinda just go with the flow."

00:06:37.200 --> 00:06:41.200
While Mintz-Plasse isn't headlining&nbsp;
big studio comedies these days,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:41.200 --> 00:06:45.280
he does frequently show up for a supporting&nbsp;
role or cameo appearance in movies involving&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:45.280 --> 00:06:50.720
his Superbad castmate Seth Rogen. These gigs&nbsp;
keep Mintz-Plasse in the public eye and allow&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:50.720 --> 00:06:54.880
him to stay busy making the kind of movies&nbsp;
that made him famous in the first place. They&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:54.880 --> 00:06:59.920
also keep him around as a solid part of Rogen's&nbsp;
prolific and lucrative filmmaking enterprise.

00:06:59.920 --> 00:07:05.680
For example, in 2017's The Disaster Artist, which&nbsp;
Rogen co-produced and had a supporting role in,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:05.680 --> 00:07:10.960
Mintz-Plasse has a small, uncredited role.&nbsp;
He had a bigger part in 2013's apocalyptic&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:10.960 --> 00:07:16.160
comedy This is the End, in which Rogen and his&nbsp;
usual gang of collaborators play heightened,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:16.160 --> 00:07:19.440
terrible versions of themselves&nbsp;
who lose their minds as the world&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:19.440 --> 00:07:24.560
ends. Mintz-Plasse shows up as himself to be&nbsp;
sucked into Hell. Rogen even let Mintz-Plasse&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:24.560 --> 00:07:30.480
deliver a completely absurd male anatomy joke&nbsp;
in 2014's Neighbors, in which he played Scoonie,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:30.480 --> 00:07:34.720
a ridiculously well-endowed young man.&nbsp;
It seems that as long as Mintz-Plasse's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:34.720 --> 00:07:38.160
colleagues are still making movies, they'll&nbsp;
often have a part with his name on it.

00:07:38.800 --> 00:07:42.080
You may not have seen Mintz-Plasse too&nbsp;
much lately, but there's a good chance&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:42.080 --> 00:07:46.240
you've heard him. He's been a go-to player&nbsp;
for DreamWorks Animation for a while now,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:46.240 --> 00:07:51.040
ever since he landed the role of thoughtful&nbsp;
Viking Fishlegs in 2010's How to Train Your&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:51.040 --> 00:07:55.600
Dragon. He's gone on to reprise that role in&nbsp;
multiple sequels and TV shows, and he worked&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:55.600 --> 00:08:00.240
with DreamWorks again on the Trolls franchise&nbsp;
as the voice of the villainous King Gristle.

00:08:00.240 --> 00:08:05.200
Mintz-Plasse no longer sports the whiny voice&nbsp;
he utilized with great skill as McLovin.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:05.200 --> 00:08:08.720
He's in fact developed into a skilled&nbsp;
and versatile voice-over performer,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:08.720 --> 00:08:12.400
playing characters in the likes of such&nbsp;
projects as Marmaduke and ParaNorman.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:12.400 --> 00:08:16.400
Becoming an in-demand voice actor isn't a&nbsp;
bad thing by any stretch. It's great work&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:16.400 --> 00:08:21.120
for an actor with a particular skill set, and&nbsp;
Mintz-Plasse's enthusiastic vocal performances&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:21.120 --> 00:08:25.920
indicate that he loves doing it. Entire careers&nbsp;
can unfold within the realm of voice acting that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:25.920 --> 00:08:31.120
are just as lucrative, creatively challenging,&nbsp;
and memorable as ones rooted in live-action.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:31.120 --> 00:08:36.000
But even with all these positive attributes, voice&nbsp;
acting is simply not going to get Mintz-Plasse's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:36.000 --> 00:08:40.560
face on the radar of Hollywood casting agents&nbsp;
the same way that live-action parts would.

00:08:41.360 --> 00:08:44.000
Five years after Superbad&nbsp;
exploded onto the big screen,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:44.000 --> 00:08:48.720
Mintz-Plasse was given an opportunity to break&nbsp;
out of his McLovin image while still remaining&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:48.720 --> 00:08:54.240
within the fold of big Hollywood comedies.&nbsp;
In 2012, he joined the cast of Get a Job,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:54.240 --> 00:08:59.200
which was helmed by Rodger Dodger director&nbsp;
Dylan Kidd. Mintz-Plasse was cast as Ethan,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:59.200 --> 00:09:03.680
a housemate of the main character Will, played&nbsp;
by Miles Teller. Will is a recent college&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:03.680 --> 00:09:08.000
graduate who doesn't quite know what to do with&nbsp;
himself professionally upon being thrust into&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:08.000 --> 00:09:12.080
adulthood. Ethan winds up being the most&nbsp;
financially, if dubiously, successful&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:12.080 --> 00:09:17.280
member of Will's social group when he designs&nbsp;
a popular smartphone app that enables stalkers.

00:09:17.280 --> 00:09:20.160
"Say there's a person that you wanna find, but&nbsp;
they don't wanna be found, right? Okay? Like&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:20.160 --> 00:09:24.640
there's this girl, and you've called her a million&nbsp;
times, and she just won't return your messages."

00:09:24.640 --> 00:09:30.000
Unfortunately, Get a Job didn't do much to advance&nbsp;
Mintz-Plasse's momentum at a critically important&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:30.000 --> 00:09:35.520
stage in his career. Distribution issues initially&nbsp;
delayed its release by two years, and ultimately,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:35.520 --> 00:09:41.040
it wouldn't be available to the public until&nbsp;
2016. That's a whopping four years after it&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:41.040 --> 00:09:46.560
was filmed in 2012, a whole bachelor's degree's&nbsp;
worth of time. To add insult to injury, critics&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:46.560 --> 00:09:52.320
seriously hated the movie, with Dan Mecca of The&nbsp;
Film Stage calling it, quote, "a brutally cynical,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:52.320 --> 00:09:57.760
largely unfunny film." It was released straight&nbsp;
to home video in the United States, though it did&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:57.760 --> 00:10:03.680
play to theatergoers in South Africa, where it&nbsp;
earned a little more than $23,000. Mintz-Plasse&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:03.680 --> 00:10:07.920
certainly got a job making this movie, but&nbsp;
it didn't really lead to any further ones.

00:10:08.720 --> 00:10:13.760
One reason for Mintz-Plasse's low visibility in&nbsp;
Hollywood is that he seems to genuinely prefer&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:13.760 --> 00:10:18.320
things this way. Between his private nature,&nbsp;
his choice to pursue supporting roles,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:18.320 --> 00:10:23.200
his frequent voice acting gigs, and his musical&nbsp;
pursuits, there's little sign that he even wants&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:23.200 --> 00:10:28.160
to headline blockbusters or take the lead in a&nbsp;
major network sitcom. But if he ever does want&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:28.160 --> 00:10:32.320
to pursue either of those things, it's definitely&nbsp;
not too late for him to do so. He's got the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:32.320 --> 00:10:37.200
acting chops to make it happen, not to mention the&nbsp;
goodwill that comes from co-starring in Superbad,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:37.200 --> 00:10:42.240
an enduringly popular millennial touchstone.&nbsp;
A career is never really dead in Hollywood;&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:42.240 --> 00:10:44.000
it just needs the right jump-start.

00:10:44.000 --> 00:10:48.960
And in recent years, Mintz-Plasse has finally&nbsp;
started shedding the nerd roles so commonly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:48.960 --> 00:10:53.120
offered to him. In fact, his career has&nbsp;
taken recent turns into more artistic&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:53.120 --> 00:10:59.200
and idiosyncratic territory. In 2019, he was&nbsp;
hired to play a small but pivotal role as a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:59.200 --> 00:11:05.200
creepy guy in 2020’s satirical thriller Promising&nbsp;
Young Woman, which starred Carey Mulligan as a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:05.200 --> 00:11:10.480
woman seeking to avenge her best friend by&nbsp;
preying on men who claim to be nice guys.

00:11:10.480 --> 00:11:11.680
"I just thought that you were-"

00:11:11.680 --> 00:11:12.240
"Drunk?"

00:11:12.240 --> 00:11:12.800
"Yeah."

00:11:12.800 --> 00:11:14.120
"Really drunk?"

00:11:14.120 --> 00:11:15.600
"F---. Yeah."

00:11:15.600 --> 00:11:19.040
"Well, I'm not. But that's good, isn't it?"

00:11:19.040 --> 00:11:20.080
"I think you should leave."

00:11:20.080 --> 00:11:24.160
And Mintz-Plasse has also been getting&nbsp;
weird by voicing "Son" in the grotesquely&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:24.160 --> 00:11:28.800
puppeteered Comedy Central series Blark&nbsp;
and Son. So in reality, Mintz-Plasse is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:28.800 --> 00:11:33.280
still hanging with comedy's cool kids, just&nbsp;
in a slightly different way than he used to.

00:11:33.280 --> 00:11:35.520
Check out one of our newest&nbsp;
videos right here! Plus,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:35.520 --> 00:11:38.560
even more Looper videos about your&nbsp;
favorite stars are coming soon.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:38.560 --> 00:11:49.600
Subscribe to our YouTube channel and hit&nbsp;
the bell so you don't miss a single one.

